{"activity_text": "Anti-Gram-, Anti-inflammatory", "article_id": "", "assay_text": "Unknown", "assay_type": "", "comments_text": "Sequence analysis: APD analysis reveals that this sequence is similar (80.77%) to TAT-KR-12 R: 38%; K: 17%. Activity: active against E. coli (MIC 12.09 uM), P. aeruginosa (MIC 6,12 uM), S.aureus (MIC > 320 uM). Similar to KR-12, the peptide is also anti-inflammatory by supressing the cytokine levels of TNF-alpha, IL-1beta, and IL-6 from PS-stimulated RAW264.7 cells. cf. CPP is unable to do so. MOA:bacteria:E. coli: MOA:bacteria:P. aeruginosa: membrane permeation. peptide formulation:Silica precipitation:CPP-KR12@Si: both KR-12 and CPP-KR12 are able to precipitate silica to form silica particles, allowing controlled delivery. The peptide retained its activity against Gran-negative bacteria and became more active against S. aureus after formulation.", "concentration": "", "cytotoxicity_text": "", "hemolytic_activity_text": "", "measure_group": "", "measure_value": "", "pubmed_id": "38003614", "record_granularity": "entry_text", "reference_text": "Int J Mol Sci. 2023 Nov 16;24(22):16423. doi: 10.3390/ijms242216423. PubMed", "sequence_key": "APD6:AP04797", "source_id": "AP04797", "source_numeric_id": "04797", "source_path": "APD6/apd6_export/structured/peptides.csv", "source_record_id": "AP04797", "source_table": "peptides.csv", "stability_text": "", "subject_name": "", "target_organism_text": "", "title": "Self-Entrapment of Antimicrobial Peptides in Silica Particles for Stable and Effective Antimicrobial Peptide Delivery System", "unit": "", "﻿database": "APD6"}
